    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Tuberculosis (TB) is an infectious disease caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis (Mtb) and is one of the top ten causes of death worldwide. Current diagnostic methods available in low resource settings take days to weeks to perform or suffer from poor sensitivity. Previously, members of the Bertozzi group attached a solvatochromic fluorophore, 4-N,N-dimethylamino-1,8-naphthalimide (DMN), to the sugar trehalose (Tre) for incorporation into the mycomembrane, a hydrophobic layer of the cell envelope formed by trehalose glycolipids unique to Mtb and closely related organisms. Solvatochromic dyes shift in color or intensity based on the electronic properties of the surrounding solvent; DMN is fluorescent in hydrophobic but not hydrophilic environments. Thus, DMN-Tre does not label bacterial species that lack trehalose glycolipids because the dye fluoresces strongly in the mycomembrane but not in the surrounding solution. For this honors research study, we synthesized and characterized new solvatochromic trehalose probes (3HC-2-Tre and 3HC-3-Tre) that improve upon the properties of DMN-Tre. We performed in vitro labeling studies of mycobacteria to determine specificity and found that 3HC-2-Tre and 3HC-3-Tre can be used at concentrations 10x lower than DMN-Tre, are much brighter with less background fluorescence, and, in the case of 3HC-3-Tre, can label bacteria within 1 minute without requiring a wash step. 3HC-2-Tre is highly selective for bacterial species with a mycomembrane. Pre-labeled mycobacteria injected into zebrafish are readily detected by microscopy. These probes have the potential for use in TB diagnostics and the study of the effect of drug treatment on cell wall dynamics.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
